s who had  denouncing vigorously the incompetence  of the people who've been playing the  market in that wager in the period ask  them if these people were as bad as you  say they were why didn't you blow the  whistle  and their answer of course was to say  because they were willing to buy risks  at prices at which I was very happy to  sell them and what I saw had been going  on in the market during that period was  what our sins come to see as an exemplar  caricature of what goes on in financial  markets as a whole in which transactions  occur like the wallet auction like the  asymmetric information story knot in  which people who can bear risks for whom  the cost of beer is bearing a high  transfer them to people who can bear  them more cheaply but in which people  who know quite a lot about what they're  doing transfer a variety of risks to  people who know less about what they're  doing and that the process of doing that  bids up the costs of operating in the  market as a whole now when I took this  theory the second part I used to teach  was to say if you don't believe that  markets work like that you have to  believe they're like that because that  they weren't people who behave  rationally would make money at the  expense of people who behave  irrationally and that's indeed the  underpinning of the argument for modern  finance theory as a basis for financial  markets I now still believe that if  people who don't if people behave  irrationally they lose money to behave  people pay more rationally but what I  now believe is that yes that is exactly  what goes on in a lot of the risk  markets which we have and I came to see  that more clearly and another bizarre  occasion in my life London is one of the  two or three centers of the world we're  really high rolling gamblers go to lose  money there are half a dozen casinos in  London which are the kind of places  where people can go that the sorts of  people go who go who are so rich and so  addicted to gambling that if they lose a  quarter of a million dollars in an  evening that's something they can  cope with quite easily and do quite  frequently and these six claps  incredibly opulent places most of them  in Mayfair in London are where this kind  of gambling occurs now for bizarre  reasons I spend an evening in which I  was driven from one of these clubs to  the other and I learnt quite a number of  things in the course of the evening but  the one that most surprised me was that  I'd expected these to be jolly places  where people were having fun and the  people who were doing it had so much  money that if they lost a quarter of a  million bond in an evening having a good  time it didn't really matter to them but  they weren't in the main having a good  time they were sitting there grim-faced  white-knuckled watching the roulette  wheel spinning are the cards fall and  this seemed to me and still does quite  old the majority of people in these  clubs are actually people who are  successful entrepreneurial business  that's how they acquired the kinds of  fortunes which enable them to engage in  this kind of activity and yet there were  these successful entrepreneurial  businessman sitting there not just once  but evening after evening genuinely  believing that they were going to win at  Roulette and continuing to maintain that  belief despite repeated evidence that in  fact they didn't how could this be well  as I thought about it afterwards the  elements of the explanation you know  have come to me yes these people are  irrationally optimistic most successful  entrepreneurial business men are  irrationally optimistic there are people  who have taken on risks that sensible  business school trained people like you  and me you know would not dream of  taking on and which we would be right to  take on but for not to take on but for  some of these people you know these  risks come on  there are other people more people for  whom they don't come off but they're not  the people you see in Aspen holes in the  Clurman Club they're the people who are  running a garage you know having gone  bankrupt in three previous ventures to  do the same the people we see in Aspen  homes are the people who are drawn from  a very small upper tail of the  distribution but these are the people we  recognize as successful entrepreneurs in  the modern economy in other words what  I'm saying is that the functioning of  capitalism actually depends on a  constant supply of people who are  irrationally optimistic and it is from  these people that a large proportion of  the innovation both technical and  organizational that makes our market  economy function actually comes so that  this these models I used to teach in in  in economics courses and business  schools about rational economic behavior  don't describe very well this underlying  dynamic of the capitalist economy nor do  they describe well the operation of  financial markets which are in large  part institutions designed like blood  play the casino is to take some money  off these kinds of people and  redistribute it to more rational  thoughtful people like those of us in  this particular room in securities  markets in other words what generally  dominates these markets is that people  sell risks to other people who know less  about the risks that are being taken  that are being taken off it's a  different model from that one which I  used to teach and in understanding how  it is that capitalist economies depend  on the supply of irrationally optimistic  people we start to get towards what for  me is the real dynamic  of a market economy which is what I call  discipline pluralism when I described in  the book is a comparison of some of the  elements of central planning versus the  market economy I Allah straight central  planning through a variety of examples  one of the classics of which is the  story of crew scoffs maze in 1958  Khrushchev as the Cold War started to  thaw after the death of Stalin  Khrushchev paid a visit to the United  States it's a very bizarre visit in a  lot of ways one of the highlights was  when he and his aides went to a  supermarket and they went away believing  that the shelves had all been specially  stopped up you know for their arrival as  would indeed have happened if the  President of the United States that have  visited a supermarket in the Soviet  Union but what made the biggest  impression on Khrushchev was when they  went to Iowa and they were taken to see  the maize fields of the prairies there  was clearly no faking the fact that the  crops of maize stretched into the  distance or as far as the eye could see  and Khrushchev returned to Russia  persuaded that the future of Soviet  agriculture rested on maize so a large  part of Soviet wheat production was  converted to maize the experiment it  must be said was not a success there  were good reasons why it was wheat  rather than maize that was the  traditional crop but this was not what  Khrushchev was told he was told that the  experiment was going well and to the  extent that it wasn't it was because of  sabotage by people who were  insufficiently committed under socialism  one of his acolytes famously said maize  can be grown anywhere  but it couldn't and the reduction in  Soviets agricultural production was  actually one of the reasons for the  economic setback that eventually led to  led to Khrushchev being toppled from  power now the idea of experimenting with  maize in the Soviet Union was not a bad  idea what was wrong was two things one  was the scale of the experiment if an  individual Soviet farmer had gone to a  Mary and been impressed by maize he  would have gone home planted one or two  fields of maize if the experiment had  failed that would have been the end of  it if it had succeeded other people  would have imitated the experiment in  Russia was undertaken on a much larger  scale and also there was no honest  feedback about it the process by which  people tell their superiors how well  their ideas are going as they're  implemented in practice is one which I  suspect is familiar not just in the  Soviet Union but to most of you in this  room who've spent any part of your your  working careers in large corporations  it's the combination of scale of  decision making and the absence of  honest feedback in contrast with the  story of discipline pluralism which is  it best exemplified going back to  Redmond by the evolution of personal  computers in the United States the key  thing about the story of the computer  industry in in America is that there  wasn't any single voice it's actually  the product it's not the product of the  vision of Bill Gates or Steve Jobs or  any other heroic individual indeed most  of the experiments which were made in  the course of the development of pcs  failed even most of the most of the  experiments which turned out to be  technically important to the development  of the industry failed for failed  commercially for the people who  implemented them and pioneered them  probably the company which has made the  single most important contributions to  the development of pcs was Xerox which  manufactured in 1973 the first thing  that anyone would recognize as a  personal computer and later invented the  graphical user interface the systems are  mouse and icons which produce the key  development that we now have computers  that you don't have to know anything  about computers in order to be able to  use but Xerox failed commercially with  both of these and and in terms of  profits Xerox gained nothing out of  being such an important contributor to  the personal computer revolution  discipline pluralism is for me the key  attribute of a more market organization  a system in which there is extensive  experiment and in which unsuccessful  experiment is ultimately cut cut off  it's the process that is motivated by  the kind of irrationally optimistic  entrepreneurs I was describing earlier  the people I saw amass panels and the  clairement so I want to argue that how  market economies function is a more  complex story than the one which is told  in the American business model not only  a more complex story but a much more  interesting story as well I'm going to  pass briefly over the slides which I  brought to America to annoy people  which are the ones in witches will  describe earlier I spend most of my  working life now in France and I have  this set of slides that explains how the  French economy is actually in many  respects superior in terms of its  performance to the American one I won't  develop that thesis at least at this  time this morning what I will do simply  is to take this illustration you know of  a French market to emphasize that  markets function by virtue of their  social context motivation needs to be  seen in a social context market  institutions deal with a range of  problems like information asymmetry  through the variety of reputations and  trust relationships that surround the  operation of a market economy we can  deal with people with confidence because  we develop relations between them and  it's relationships not contracts that  actually enforce the transactions and  the activities of the market economy the  market economy which we have today in  the 20 or so rich successful societies  of the world is the product of two  hundred years of coevolution of  institutions technology politics and  society and it's essential and central  that all these things have evolved and  developed together and it's when we  understand that that we understand why  simple attempts at mimicking the  institutions of a market economy fail  and why when we go to Russia or Iraq or  to some of the poor countries of the  world with a belief that there's a  simple manual of our market economy  functions and if you only follow that  you will develop successful economic  institutions why these attempts not only  fail but fail by orders of magnitude  without that kind of simultaneous  evolution of social cultural political  institutions with market institutions  market institutions will not actually  function effectively you may have seen  on one of the history slides with which  I began this presentation the slogan  capitalism will be should be replaced by  something nicer a slogan which we took  we saw on the banners of some of the  Mayday protesters in London a couple of  years ago I think it encapsulates better  than better than I could in much longer  sentences the basic incoherence of  today's modern critics of capitalism but  it's not very surprising that market  economies and capitalism are unpopular  if we describe capitalism and market  economies as encouraging people to be  very greedy and imposing as few  restrictions on what as possible on what  they do that's not just not how a market  economy works it's not even a possible  description of how a market economy  could be made to work and the more we  make our real market economies like that  the worse they actually function the odd  thing that has happened in my mind in  the 10 15 years since the end of the  Cold War is that we presented those of  us in economics and business we've  presented to people outside a  description of the market economy that  is succeed simultaneously in being both  repellent and false when capitalism  should be replaced by something nicer I  want to suggest that that nicer thing  with which capitalism should be replaced  is actually a better description of how  it is that a market economy actually  functions thank you all very much  you'll notice on your seat that there  are plenty of pins and if any of you  have questions if you'd write them down  right now and and pass them down to this  edge they'll be collected or to either  edge they'll be collected and we'll have  a chance to ask John at least some of  them and John I want to start off with  some questions that I got from your book  and as these other questions come in the  first one is that your book seems to say  that rich societies have cultures that  and political systems that support  mutual benefit and that poor societies  don't and I'm wondering if you could  illuminate what some of the what that  mutual benefit ethos is and and how it  works in some cases and not in others  there are loads of elements to the  coevolution of institutions that I've  described that makes up a market economy  these range from what perhaps for me is  one of the most fundamental requirements  of the successful market economy which  is what I describe as disinterested  government that is that the nature of  government should cease to be what it  has been for most of history and still  is in most countries of the world today  a mechanism by which the people who gain  control of the government are able to  gain control of the resources of the  country for their own benefit the  transforming element that made a market  economy possible or certainly without  that transforming element it could not  have become possible was the  developments of systems of government in  Europe in the United States that didn't  have that kind of characteristic and we  all know even from the United States  today that actually maintaining  disinterested government of that kind is  something that damar  constant vigilance so that's a one  prerequisite but equally let me go from  the sublime to the ridiculous if you go  and have a beer after work in a bar  you'll be handed a glass of beer that  means you're entitled to the beer but  you're expected to give the glass back  you can also sit at a table but your  right to sit at a table is something you  have to replenish from time to time in  the course of an evening by buying  another drink another round of drinks  it's also very common as you know to  engage in this practice of buying rounds  of drinks now we know all these things  but if you imagine coming from Mars you  know and go walking into a bar or even  imagine yourselves coming from a you  know as a subsistence farmer from a poor  country and finding yourself in a bar  you would not know all these things you  would have to have them explained to you  and that's just a very simple example of  how every transaction we engage in in a  market economy even the most basic you  know like getting something to drink is  surrounded by a set of social rules and  conventions that have evolved over  hundreds of years that we all take for  granted but we shouldn't take for  granted because to create a successful  functioning market economy you have to  create all these rules and conventions  and that's why you can't write the  simple manual what has to be as you  describe a blended mixed well the next  is a question you're going to love how  would you advise bush or in the event he  has a successor to develop Iraq's  economy  right well I'm sure you all know the  Irish directions of if I was going there  I wouldn't start from years I mean it is  quite hard and I still find it slightly  hard to credit you know the naivety of  the belief which genuinely does seem to  motivated at least some supporters of  that intervention that there is a very  simple story about how liberal democracy  and market economies work and not only  that everyone wants these things they  want the results of these things it's  not so clear they want the institutions  that everyone wants these things and if  they only only they read the book they  will be able to set up these  institutions we can't design a set of  market economy and democratic  institutions for Iraq or for for any  other country and those countries like  perhaps my own that have a longer  history of imperialism than the United  States have learnt that by painful  experience you know decades ago so it's  not it's not a possible opportunity for  America to create a functioning market  economy in Iraq that has to be very  largely done by the Iraqi people  themselves in order to achieve that a  whole variety of other political and  cultural preconditions need to be in  place and that development is something  that will you know will take a very long  time the notion that we could establish  as it were a beacon of market economics  and democracy in the Middle East from  which everyone else would learn was  simply a very naive mistake that's not  an answer but it's it's a response  that's great just stay with us here if  you were going to try to give advice to  third world countries or developing  nations you mentioned in your book  there's a relatively few that are really  prosperous on on a growth track what  advice would you give their their  leaders I mean the answer once again to  that is this is a lengthy process of  coevolution you know and it can't be  very easily kick-started we hat right I  described in the book the remarkable  fact that they're not very into me many  intermediate countries in the world  there are as I described about 20 or so  rich countries there's a mass of poor  countries and there are very few in  between there are some of the poorer  countries of Europe like Greece and  Portugal countries like South Korea and  Taiwan which are on the way up to being  rich states but they're not very many of  these intermediate countries and the  reason is that you need all these sets  of preconditions in place in order to do  it now the most optimistic area for  development has been and is Southeast  Asia and that's in large part because a  lot of these other conditions you know  were already in place in a sense and  risk the way I put it in the book one of  the biggest questions of economic  history one of the biggest difficulties  is to understand why it was that two  three hundred years ago economic  development took off in Western Europe  rather than South East China because in  many technological resource respects  these societies at that time looked  similar so in some sense is when we look  at China today we are not led to be  surprised less that their current growth  rate than at the fact that China has  actually been so poor for so long an  observation which is reinforced by  noticing our successful Chinese people  have been economically outside China  whether in Hong Kong Singapore  taijuan or in the united states and  britain so that to reinforce again the  point that we need all these political  cultural conditions to be a right to be  in place poor countries have to develop  like the iraqis these institutions for  themselves over period a long period of  time we can give them bits of advice we  can also give them specific humanitarian  assistance but there are very large  limits to what we in rich countries can  do and it's an important observation to  make but another one that demands  another lengthy answer that we are not  rich in rich countries because poor  people in poor countries are poor or  vice versa  and it's very important that both rich  and poor should understand that and that  what makes us in rich countries so  prosperous is essentially the high  levels of productivity that we enjoy  domestically John one of the  observations you make in the book is  that Americans lead the world in  personal consumption by a very large  measure and also they also lead the  world in the shortest vacations of  longest working hours and I'm wondering  if you might comment on that and how  that might have come to be and you're  inviting me to get into the the America  versus France story let me let me give  you a little bottom line on that which  is to say that I don't expect many  Americans to believe this but  productivity in terms of output per hour  is actually higher in France than it is  in the United States however personal  consumption in the United States per  head is 60% higher than that of France  now a whole variety of factors lead to  that difference you know right one is  that the Americans run a very large  trade deficit you as a nation consume  consider  more than you produce and the French  don't another is that government  expenditure in France is a lot higher  than it is in the United States but the  largest difference is the comparison in  working hours and let me simply give you  you know these figures to illustrate  well to remarkable facts one is how much  longer it is that people work in the  United States than they do in European  countries the other is that while in  almost all European countries the number  of hours which people work has been  falling quite fast right in the United  States average working hours has  actually been going up and the United  States is actually a complete outlier in  this both in the length of time people  work and live in the fact that it's  rising all experienced from elsewhere in  the world is that the richer people get  the less work they do there are three  parts to what's going on one is  Americans of longer working weeks and  other that Americans have less holiday  and another is that Americans retire  later you know one American man in -  between 60 and 65 is working one  Frenchman and six between 60 and 65 is  working amazingly to a European one  American man in six between 70 and 75 is  working I think the only Frenchman over  70 was working is President Chirac and  the only reason he's continuing to do it  is that if you left the Alesi Palace  it's very likely he would leave it to go  to jail you know as the corruption  investigations around him gathered pace  you know these Arora will write the  these are very different social choices  but if you want a simple explanation of  the difference between French and  American economic performance it is that  the French take lunch and have five  weeks holiday  thank you John it's time to conclude and  I first of all I want to say thank you  to John Kay and  we appreciate you coming all this way  just for us John we'll be in the back  signing books after after we break up  from here I'd also like again to  acknowledge our sponsors BearingPoint  graduates club the Johnson School of  Management at Cornell University and of  course Business Week we look forward to  seeing you next time when Deepak Chopra  will speak on the soul of leadership  John Kay is a professor at the London  Business School and the University of  Oxford he writes a weekly column for the  Financial Times culture and prosperity  is published by Harper Business an  imprint of HarperCollins visit  HarperCollins com for more information
 

